{{$NEXT}}

  - the distribution builds with dzil build: LICENSE is committed, and
    README.md describes the module and both readers
  - the cpanfile pins AnyEvent (7.17) and ITM (0.002) to real minimum
    versions and declares Carp and Fcntl; PAR::Packer, which bundle.bat
    calls, is a develop dependency
  - t/cpanfile.t checks the declared dependencies and that nothing is
    pinned to 0
  - bin/itm_read_simple no longer imports Term::ANSIColor and drops its
    unused color list; it now behaves like itm_read_nocolor and starts
    without Term::ANSIColor installed
  - bin/itm_read_simple POD no longer promises colored output
  - t/scripts.t runs both shipped readers over the same packet stream
  - AnyEvent::ITM::_print_ts and AnyEvent::ITM->_run_cmd are now the
    public, documented print_ts and run_cmd; both shipped readers use the
    new names, the old underscore names are gone
  - t/run_cmd.t checks the exit code and signal that run_cmd reports
  - lib/AnyEvent/ITM.pm, bin/itm_read_simple and bin/itm_read_nocolor
    now declare their own $VERSION (0.101), all the same
  - the hand-written SUPPORT sections are gone from the POD of all three
    files; the release bundle generates them, with the channel #hardware
    now set through irc in dist.ini
  - t/version.t checks the $VERSION of every file under lib/ and bin/ and
    that no POD carries a SUPPORT section
  - the itm read type treats an empty buffer as "nothing there yet" and
    returns 0 instead of warning and reporting an overflow packet; the dead
    length and byte values and the unreachable "unknown packet type" croak
    are gone from the reader
  - handle() and run_cmd() croak instead of die, so the message names the
    caller's file and line
  - itm_read_simple and itm_read_nocolor accept a file named 0
  - t/reader.t pins the framing rules (empty buffer, partial packet, one
    packet per read), the croak locations and a file named 0
  - GitHub Actions CI runs the suite and the author tests on Linux with
    the newest stable Perl; xt/ holds a perlcritic test (severity 5) and
    a POD coverage test that understands the =method shortcut
  - handle() is documented, and t/handle.t pins its file modes (pipe
    read-write, otherwise read-only, non-blocking), the EOF signal and the
    "$fatal: $message" error signal
